   "A": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 402 of the laws of 1994, amending the state administrative procedure act relating to requiring certain agencies to submit regulatory agendas for publication in the state register, in relation to extending expiration of the provisions thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7660",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7660-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §202-d, St Ad Proc Act; amd §2, Chap 402 of 1994",
    "lawSection": "State Administrative Procedure Act",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7660A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the state administrative procedure act, in relation to\nregulatory agendas; and to amend chapter 402 of the laws of 1994,\namending the state administrative procedure act relating to requiring\ncertain agencies to submit regulatory agendas for publication in the\nstate register, in relation to extending the expiration of certain\nprovisions of such chapter\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill amends the State Administrative Procedure Act (SAPA) to\nstreamline the process for print and online publication of regulatory\nagendas by state agencies.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nBill § 1 amends subdivision 1 of SAPA § 202-d to:\n\n1) Eliminate the current requirement for a second semi-annual\npublication of a regulatory agenda in June, making publication of\nregulatory agendas an annual requirement;\n\n2) Change the required timing for publication of a regulatory agenda\nfrom the first regular issue of the State Register in January to any\nissue published in January;\n\n3) Remove the option of publishing a continuously updated online\nagenda as an alternative to the now-deleted June publication -\ninstead, any agency could update its online regulatory agenda at any\ntime without triggering any additional requirement for print\npublication;\n\n4) Expand the list of agencies that are required to publish a\nregulatory agenda by adding three agencies - the Office of Mental\nHealth, the Office for People with Developmental Disabilities and the\nState Gaming Commission -- which have become significant regulators in\nrecent years; and\n\n5) Provide that a regulatory agenda may indicate if a draft version of\na potential rule is being made publicly available.\n\nSection two amends section 2 of the state administrative procedure act\nto extend the provisions requiring certain agencies to submit\nregulatory agendas for publication in the state register until 2020.\n\nSection three establishes that this act shall take effect immediately\nand shall apply to all regulatory agendas published on or after such\ndate\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nRegulatory agendas provide the public with advance notice of\nregulations that are being developed or are under consideration by a\nstate agency, but which have not yet been formally proposed.\nExperience with the provisions of SAPA § 202-d in recent years has\npointed out opportunities to streamline its provisions to make it\neasier for state agencies to provide the public with this valuable\ninformation.\n\nSpecifically, the current requirement for semi-annual publications\ndoubles the workloads of agencies without providing a substantial\nbenefit to the public. The June agendas include a high percentage of\nrepetitive information, and in some cases agencies have simply\ndispensed with the June publication entirely. Limiting the publication\nrequirement to once per year and allowing publication in any Register\nissue will make it easier for agencies to focus on a single,\ncomprehensive list of potential rules. An agency may still provide\nadditional supplements to the January publication by updating the\nregulatory agenda on its website.\n\nIn addition to the above changes to the publication process, the bill\nadds three agencies - the Office of Mental Health, the Office for\nPeople with Developmental Disabilities and the State Gaming Commission\n-- to the statutory list of entities required to publish regulatory\nagendas, due to increased regulatory activity by those three agencies.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nSAPA § 202-d currently requires several major regulatory agencies to\npublish regulatory agendas twice per year, in the first regular issue\nof the State Register published in January and the last regular issue\npublished in June. An agency may avoid the June publication by\nmaintaining a continuously updated regulatory agenda on its website.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall apply to all\nregulatory agendas published on or after such date; provided, however,\nthat the amendments to subdivision 1 of section 202-d of the state\nadministrative procedure act made by section one of this act shall not\naffect the expiration of such subdivision and shall be deemed to\nexpire therewith.\n",
